{"slug":"stablecoin-hold-sized-by-volatility","name":"Stablecoin hold sized by volatility","status":"rejected","created_at":"2026-11-15T06:16:34.694207Z","version":1,"frozen":true,"implementation":"stablecoin-vol-hold","runs":2,"trials":2,"deflated_sharpe":4.9116067946386455e-05,"asset_class":"crypto","sections":{"hypothesis":"Hypothesis","rationale":"Why the edge should exist","rules":"Rules in plain English","falsification":"What would prove it wrong","parameters":"Parameters and allowed ranges","costs":"Expected turnover against costs"},"notes":[{"version":1,"created_at":"2026-11-15T06:16:34.694207Z","sections":{"hypothesis":"Holding BTC while stablecoin supply grows (Sunday decisions), sized by min(1, 50% / 30-day volatility), beats holding BTC at the same average exposure on Sharpe and keeps the drawdown within 50%.","rationale":"The plain stablecoin hold (watch) beat holding at the same exposure on BTC and ETH, but took every crash in full (drawdown -66% and -80%) because it is fully in whenever supply grows. Volatility targeting is what turned simple trend following into the one strategy on pursue: holding less when the market is wild cuts crash losses (Moreira and Muir 2017, Volatility-Managed Portfolios). Claude's suggestion, 2026-11-15. Against it: volatility-managed holding alone failed here (below the market at the same exposure), so it may only help in combination. Its holdout year is unseen for this signal.","rules":"stablecoin-vol-hold on BTCUSDT 1d: on/off as the stablecoin hold (each Sunday close, on if stablecoins_up is 1, for the week); while on, weight = min(1, 0.5 / annualised 30-day volatility), rebalanced daily outside the 2% band. 330 USDT, costs from kestrel.toml. Period 2018-01 to 2025-10, before the holdout. Also run on ETHUSDT, reported beside it.","falsification":"The full scorecard on BTC; fails on any failed test. ETH is reported beside it; a result there below ETH held at the same exposure is a warning. A pass leads to a holdout note.